<!-- The body of the ZBuf extension a Query carries its VALUE in, whose layout
     depends on whether the extension before it was the shared-memory marker.

     Upstream writes a query's value as one ZBuf extension (`ValueType`,
     `commons/zenoh-protocol/src/zenoh/query.rs` @
     `pub type QueryBodyType = crate::zenoh::ext::ValueType<`). Its body starts
     with a length, and what follows depends on a marker extension (unit,
     id 4) that may have come just before it:

       no marker : the length is true and the value is that many bytes, which
                   the host reads as an encoding followed by the payload, as
                   `query_value_ext` does.
       marker    : the length is the LOGICAL length of the buffer, a number of
                   bytes that are not on the wire, and the body is not a run of
                   bytes but an encoding and a list of slices, each of which
                   says how long it is. Upstream computes the declared length
                   from the buffer's logical size
                   (`commons/zenoh-codec/src/core/zbuf.rs` @
                   `message.zslices().fold(0, |acc, x| acc + 1 + x.len())`) and
                   its reader does not use it to END the body: it subtracts the
                   bytes the encoding took and requires the result not to be
                   negative (`commons/zenoh-codec/src/zenoh/mod.rs` @
                   `header.len.checked_sub(start - end)`), then reads the slices
                   by what each of them says. So the declared length is on the
                   wire in both layouts, is read in both, and ends the body in
                   one only. A reader that trusts it in the marker case reads
                   past the frame, which is how the shared-memory value of a
                   query ended a wz session.

     The two layouts are the two arms of one flag-input, `after_shm`, which is
     1 exactly when the extension before this one was the marker. The entry
     codec hands it down from its own flag-input and the chain that reads the
     entries supplies it (`query.scxml`), because only the chain knows what
     came before an entry.

     The slice list is the one a Put's payload uses when its extension chain
     carries the marker (`zbuf_slice.scxml`: kind, length, bytes), and the
     encoding is the same `encoding` codec the Put's E-gated embed uses, so
     the two layouts of a value agree about both.

     Wire shape:
       byte 0+     : value_len    (VLE u64, declared; read in both layouts)
       !after_shm  : value        (length=value_len, max 32)
       after_shm   : encoding     (the encoding codec),
                     slice_count  (VLE u32),
                     slices       (that many `zbuf_slice` elements) -->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="codec" sce:default-endian="big" name="query_value_zbuf">
  <sce:import src="encoding.scxml" kind="codec" as="codec_zenoh_encoding"/>
  <sce:import src="zbuf_slice.scxml" kind="codec" as="codec_zenoh_zbuf_slice"/>
  <sce:flag-inputs>
    <sce:flag-input name="after_shm" width="1"/>
  </sce:flag-inputs>
  <datamodel>
    <sce:field id="value_len" sce:type="uint64" sce:byte="0" sce:bit-size="vle"/>
    <sce:field id="value" sce:type="bytes" sce:byte="1" sce:bit-size="length-ref"
               sce:length-field="value_len" sce:max-size="32"
               sce:present-if="!after_shm"/>
    <sce:embed id="encoding" type="codec_zenoh_encoding" sce:byte="2"
               sce:present-if="after_shm"/>
    <sce:field id="slice_count" sce:type="uint32" sce:byte="3" sce:bit-size="vle"
               sce:present-if="after_shm"/>
    <sce:repeat id="slices" type="codec_zenoh_zbuf_slice" sce:byte="4"
                count="slice_count" max-count="4"
                sce:present-if="after_shm"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
